    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8ffcdd3e_aea98b9f",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/ClipStack_graphite.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1144,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-07-12T15:33:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We should take this branch if `rendererOutset` is not 0, and then add `rendererOutset` to `localStyleOutset` instead of calling `transformedShapeBounds` above. \n\nIf we update following condition at line 1148 (!isFillStyle) to be true when `rendererOutset` is not 0, then we can get rid of the Shape::setOutset entirely and not have to modify `styledShape` down below. I think that\u0027s my preferred solution since it keeps Shape just a geometry holder.\n\nAnother way of applying these outsets, instead of adding the rendererOutset to the localStyleOutset and changing `transformedShapeBounds`, would be to set `styledShape` to `transformedShapeBounds.makeOutset(rendererOutset)`. Then after we\u0027ve transformed `transformedShapeBounds` to device space (line 1155) we add a constant outset of 1px. This provides slightly tighter device-space bounds than what we\u0027d get by mapping the local rendererOutset by the local-to-device transform (since AnalyticRRectRenderStep calculates a conservative radius that ensures the final device-space radius is at least 1px, that it uses when moving vertices into the shape, but uses a separate approach that ensures outset vertices are exactly 1px out). This might couple the ClipStack to the details of AnalyticRRectRenderStep too much, though, while just relying on the local rendererOutset returned by the Renderer\u0027s virtual function avoids that issue.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1144,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 1144,
        "endChar": 32
      },
      "revId": "50f40605be4b53c53179fc55dcffa148c3dca1d4",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
